course: dictionary
lesson: 6711
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word offence has three meanings, and the sentence around it tells you which one to use. Look for clues before you choose. «A serious first doping offence can bring a ban of up to 4 years.» Here an offence is a crime, an act that breaks a rule or a law.
explain: Now look at this: «No offence, but» is said before you say something that might upset someone. The words after it are likely to hurt. So offence here means a feeling of being upset or insulted, not a crime.
explain: In a football match, the offence is the team that is attacking and trying to score. In the US, the spelling is offense, but the meaning is the same. Ask yourself, "Which meaning fits the sentence?" Then swap your meaning in and check that it still makes sense.
fact: An offence is a crime or an act that breaks a law or a rule.
fact: A minor offence is a small one, such as dropping litter. A serious offence is a much bigger crime.
fact: To take offence means to feel insulted or upset by what someone says or does.
fact: To cause offence means to upset someone, and «no offence» is said before something that might upset them.
fact: In sport, the offence is the team attacking and trying to score, the opposite of the defence.
exercise: «A serious first doping offence can bring a ban of up to 4 years.» What is an offence here? | *A crime that breaks a rule | A kind of cake | A soft, warm blanket | A happy party
exercise: «No offence, but» warns that what comes next will offend. What does this phrase tell you? | *Something upsetting may be coming | Everything is perfect | A prize is on its way | Someone is about to sing a song
exercise: In a football match, what is the offence? | The team defending their own goal | The crowd cheering in the stands | *The team attacking and trying to score | The referee in the middle of the pitch
test: Kofi said «no offence» before telling Lily her drawing was messy. What is most likely to happen next? | Kofi will say something kind and perfect | Lily will forget the drawing | Lily will win a prize | *Lily may feel upset or insulted
test: Sam laughed at the joke, but Maya began to take offence. What does take offence mean? | Start a new game | Give a present to a friend | Run away very fast | *Feel insulted or upset
exam: What is a minor offence? | A very long holiday | A goal scored in extra time | *A small crime, such as dropping litter | A kind of hot drink
